<p>A claim that drags on for months costs more than patience. Roofs stay tarped, drywall stays open, and businesses lose revenue. A <strong>delayed insurance claim in San Marcos</strong> usually stalls for identifiable reasons: a backlog after a big storm, an adjuster reassignment, a document request nobody answered, or an investigation that never seems to finish. As a San Marcos public adjuster, we help property owners figure out where the claim is stuck, what the insurer still needs, and what Texas timelines generally apply.</p>

<h2>Why Claims Get Delayed</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Cause of delay</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>What may help</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Storm backlog</td><td>Inspections scheduled weeks out after a widespread hail or wind event</td><td>Thorough photos and mitigation receipts submitted early</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adjuster turnover</td><td>New adjuster asks for information you already provided</td><td>A written log of every submission with dates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Open document requests</td><td>Insurer waits on proof of loss, receipts, or a recorded statement</td><td>Written confirmation of exactly what is outstanding</td></tr>
<tr><td>Engineer or expert review</td><td>Weeks pass waiting on a report</td><td>Ask when the report is expected and request a copy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coverage questions</td><td>A reservation of rights letter or long silence</td><td>Policy review; attorney consult if coverage is in question</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scope disagreement</td><td>Back-and-forth over estimates with no decision</td><td>A detailed line-item estimate that addresses each difference</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>San Marcos and Hays County see bursts of claims after major spring storms. NOAA's records for May 9, 2024, for example, include <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181388" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3-inch hail west of San Marcos</a> and 70 mph gusts that downed trees in the city, and the Hays County Judge signed a local disaster declaration afterward. When thousands of policyholders report at once, response times often slip. Our article on <a href="/why-property-insurance-claims-are-delayed-texas/">why property insurance claims are delayed in Texas</a> goes deeper into these patterns.</p>

<h2>Texas Prompt Payment Timelines</h2>
<p>The Texas Prompt Payment of Claims Act, in <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insurance Code Chapter 542, Subchapter B</a>, sets general deadlines for most property insurers:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>General timeframe</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Acknowledge the claim, begin investigating, request information</td><td>Within 15 days after notice of the claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accept or reject the claim</td><td>Within 15 business days after receiving all requested items</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extension if the insurer needs more time</td><td>Insurer must notify you; then up to 45 additional days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay after accepting</td><td>Within 5 business days after notifying you of acceptance</td></tr>
<tr><td>After a declared weather catastrophe</td><td>Deadlines generally extended by 15 days</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>These are general rules. Eligible surplus lines insurers follow different timeframes, and the clock for a decision usually starts only after the insurer receives everything it reasonably requested. That is why keeping track of requests and responses matters so much. Public adjusters do not give legal advice; questions about what a missed deadline means for your rights belong with a Texas attorney.</p>

